ormation  might be potentially used for more  nefarious means  such as espionage tiktok has said that  the chinese government has never asked  the company for user data  and would refuse such a request the  company also said its data on american  users is held in servers outside of  china  in the u.s and singapore there are also  concerns about freedom of expression  among the fun quirky videos uploaded to  tiktok every day  some users say that some videos have  been censored  the journal found that in the last two  years employees at tiktok followed rules  that included striking out content  that was in line with censorship norms  in china for example  showing tattoos or excessive cleavage  and politically sensitive topics  including the hong kong protests or  tiananmen square  recently the app has been censoring  other types of content too  we found one u.s user who said she was  banned from her account because  she had kissed her girlfriend on the  cheek and this happened during a live  stream  on tick tock and tick tock basically  told her after  that her account was banned for serious  pornography tick tock said that  moderation decisions are in an effort to  keep the tone on the app  light-hearted and not in the name of  censorship  tick-tock isn't facing only scrutiny  from the u.s tick-tock  is facing a possible ban in australia  the dutch data protection agency  is launching an investigation into  tick-tock where pressure grows tick-tock  is taking measures to distance itself  from its chinese roots  there's now an american ceo at the head  of tick-tock  his name is kevin mayer and he comes  from disney and by dance is also  considering other changes  to its corporate structure people  familiar with the discussions  tell us that bike dance is thinking of  establishing a headquarters for  tick-tock  outside of china as the u.s and china  clash over coronavirus  citizens rights in hong kong and  xinjiang the south china sea  and trade tech has become a major 